+# Airline warning and QFR originals: source qualification and retrieval targets
+
+October 29, 2026. Follow-up to TI05 and TEL02/TEL04. No dated AAL answer, original message export or August9 letter was recovered. The advance is a more precise original-record route and a source-based qualification of the warning/information-control lead. Earlier public findings and conflicting accounts remain unchanged.
+
+## What the held report actually adds
+
+The [Commission staff report, PDF p96/note285](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=96), preserves several different accounts:
+
+- Operations-center interviewees said management did not request that dispatchers warn airborne crews about hijackings/cockpit intrusions.
+- The airline reportedly said management did instruct dispatchers to contact aircraft with diversion instructions for the airspace shutdown.
+- The report cites an **August9,2004 AAL letter** saying individual dispatchers improvised warnings to certain aircraft. It describes a **9:22** vigilance message referring to WTC reports and unconfirmed AA-aircraft information, and a more explicit **9:36a.m.** hijacking/high-alert message.
+
+These statements concern different decisions, people, message contents and scopes. Preserve them together. An assertion that no pilots were warned would go beyond this record. Conversely, two reported dispatcher messages do not establish a management-wide warning program, complete coverage, operational receipt by a specific crew or timely delivery. The original letter, message exports, recipient/distribution records and clocks remain missing.
+
+This is a dependent source chain: **staff report → reported later airline letter → quoted earlier messages**. We read the staff-report scan, not the underlying letter or native messages. Neither the report's time labels nor its account of sending calibrates a switching, ACARS or cockpit-receipt clock. Note285 must not be expanded into evidence of what every aircraft received or what any warning could have prevented.
+
+[The predecessor TI05 is preserved exactly](PREVIOUS-TI05.json). It already distinguished no received Herndon request from no management decision and required timely-warning evidence. This supplement adds the specific contrary/contextual source passage; it does not attribute an absolute no-warnings claim to the predecessor or overwrite it.
+
+## Lockout context is also present
+
+[Report pp12–13](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/staff-report-sept2005.pdf#page=12) describes the Flight11 lockout as a standard safety/security procedure, intended to isolate information for senior management, prevent alteration or release, and protect passenger/crew identities. Page96/note285 reports the airline invoking ACSSP need-to-know requirements.
+
+Those are the report's description and the airline's reported policy account. We have not acquired the controlling policy version or tested actual compliance. This context weighs against treating the reported containment instruction or manifest restriction, by themselves, as unexplained suppression. It does not establish proper application, appropriate scope, timely warnings or the absence of obstruction elsewhere. Policy, access/instruction logs and actual dispatch distribution are the discriminating records.
+
+## Concrete original-record locations
+
+The official [Team7 series](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2555805), reached from [NARA's series guide](https://www.archives.gov/research/9-11/commission-series.html), exposes these specific file-unit descriptions:
+
+| Candidate file unit | NAID | Paper container |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| [AAL letters: AA QFR responses](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2608411) | 2608411 | 18 |
+| [AAL letters: AAL correspondence](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2608412) | 2608412 | 18 |
+| [AAL letters](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2608410) | 2608410 | 18 |
+| [AAL SOC notes](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2607997) | 2607997 | 1 |
+| [AAL documents from the Dallas/Ft.Worth briefing,11/18–19/2003](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2608111) | 2608111 | 4 |
+
+Each inspected page currently says **Not Yet Available Online** and exposed no PDF link. These are archival candidates, not proof that a particular dated answer, letter, chronology or interview is inside. They are not independently confirmed contents, release denials or proof that no released mirror exists. The specifically dated April26/July7 answers and August9 letter remain TODOs.
+
+Bounded work used six initial general-web queries, two new exact counterpart queries, two collection filters and five specifically linked file-unit pages. It did not enumerate all500 series units or repeat the prior MFR downloads. The counterpart searches did not recover actual source bodies. Failure to locate a public body is recorded locally, without treating it as evidence of concealment.
+
+## Scope, preservation and next work
+
+Four held-report page images were inspected:12,13,95 and96. Note285 is on PDF96; page95 was inspected for adjacent context and did not contain that note. Two page renders were generated from the unchanged held PDF; no new PDF was downloaded. The report's SHA-256 is **9671b7afe9a45928dcbe053e567b7f830876bc31e32a1d221b39fa59493d5bbb**. No interview audio, original messages, operational clock, policy compliance or radar test was performed.
+
+[SOURCE-PINS.json](SOURCE-PINS.json) records the source role, page locators and observed archive fields. [OBSERVATIONS-AND-TODOS.json](OBSERVATIONS-AND-TODOS.json) preserves the differently scoped statements and exact missing inputs under existing parent IDs. [CHECKLIST.json](CHECKLIST.json) accounts for requirements; [TEXT-CHANGES.diff](TEXT-CHANGES.diff) records this unique path's additions against absence. Earlier reports, original sources and the immediately preceding TI05 object remain unchanged.
+
+Next: recover the actual dated AAL responses/letter and native messages through an available original-source route; do not requery the exhausted routes unchanged. Missing telephone originals stay deferred while other ready FAA/steel operational-record leads proceed. All eighteen original five-track criteria remain unfinished. Technical recorder/radar/materials testing remains with its assigned owner.
+
+The public payload contains reviewed research notes, source locators and safe selected fields. Raw PDFs, images, OCR/extracted text, unrelated catalog personal names, contacts, signatures, private case material and strategy are excluded. Commit/push/file verification is separately receipted; public publication is not historical authentication or substantive goal completion.
